WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.960
It's been quite some time since the world
saw a "G.I.

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.350
Joe" adventure on the big screen, with the
Dwayne Johnson-led "G.I.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:12.900
Joe: Retaliation" arriving back in 2013 and
making $375 million worldwide.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.710
The action-thriller wasn't a disappointment,
but it also wasn't a rip-roaring success,

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:22.320
so the future of the Paramount-owned franchise
has been uncertain — although a third film

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.840
titled "Ever Vigilant" is supposedly still
in development.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.180
But the series is now pushing forward in a
new direction by fleshing out the origin story

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:34.750
of the team's famed ninja, Snake Eyes, in
his own solo origin film.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:39.280
With "Crazy Rich Asians" star Henry Golding
leading the film as the titular hero, the

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.910
movie sure seemed like an easy win for Paramount
Pictures.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:47.170
"Snake Eyes" director Robert Shwentke and
screenwriter Evan Spiliotopoulos focus on

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.080
intense fight scenes in the film, showing
what could be done with the character in the

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:52.170
right hands.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:56.950
The first trailer quickly caught the attention
of many action fans, especially since Iko

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:01.540
Uwais — of "The Raid" fame — plays Hard
Master, a key figure in Snake Eyes' backstory.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:07.409
However, "Snake Eyes" has bombed at the box
office, only pulling in $13 million domestically

00:01:07.409 --> 00:01:12.390
alongside a measly $4 million worldwide in
the first three days of its release.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.149
So why the poor debut?

00:01:14.149 --> 00:01:17.939
Here's why "Snake Eyes" might have flopped
so badly at the box office.

00:01:17.939 --> 00:01:21.979
After plenty of buildup in advance of the
film's release, it seems like the one-two

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:27.240
punch of poor reviews for "Snake Eyes," alongside
growing concerns about the Delta variant of

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.490
COVID-19, has put off potential audiences.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:34.899
Currently, the solo spin-off is sitting at
a 42% rating on Rotten Tomatoes alongside

00:01:34.899 --> 00:01:36.969
a B- on CinemaScore.

00:01:36.969 --> 00:01:41.409
So as audiences are cautious about returning
to theaters, many fans aren't going to want

00:01:41.409 --> 00:01:45.599
to part with their hard-earned cash for a
movie they'll possibly come away from feeling

00:01:45.599 --> 00:01:46.599
shortchanged.

00:01:46.599 --> 00:01:48.230
"What is it you want?"

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:53.359
"I want it all."

00:01:53.359 --> 00:01:57.950
That's basically what Box Office Pro analyst
Shawn Robbins explained to Variety recently,

00:01:57.950 --> 00:01:58.950
saying,

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:02.930
"Moviegoing and general household economics
are both still in recovery mode.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:06.020
Potential audiences are being selective with
what they spend their money on."

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:10.240
It's possible that the film might've had a
better reception if it had debuted as a rental

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.350
on a particular streaming service.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:16.740
But it's also understandable why Paramount
chose to stick with a theatrical release,

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.242
since "Snake Eyes" was already delayed four
times throughout the last year thanks to the

00:02:20.242 --> 00:02:22.340
ongoing global situation.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:26.180
There's also the problem of giving a team
player his own adventure in an attempt to

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.870
carry a full reboot of the "G.I.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:28.870
Joe" franchise.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:32.450
As Franchise Entertainment Research chief
David A. Gross told Variety,

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:37.010
"When a character is spun-off in a thriller
series like this, a big drop follows."

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:38.240
Gross went on to say,

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:43.000
"Demand was simply limited here, especially
without a well-known star or ensemble to drive

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.830
interest outside the fan base."

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:47.050
So is franchise fatigue to blame?

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:50.860
It's always difficult to find the next big
franchise that will bring audiences to the

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.210
movie theater in droves.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:56.110
Ever since Marvel Studios seemingly cracked
the code of what a cinematic universe can

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:01.340
do, many studios have tried replicating the
formula with varying degrees of success.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.340
So far, "G.I.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:06.020
Joe" hasn't really managed to do the same,
even with its lengthy list of characters and

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:09.940
storylines that stretch from the animated
TV shows and movies.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:11.050
Since there hasn't been a "G.I.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:16.830
Joe" film since 2013, audiences have had no
real reason to be truly excited about a revival

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:17.900
of the franchise.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:22.130
Sure, the heavy focus on impressive stunts
and fights was meant to be the hook for "Snake

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:25.240
Eyes,'" but apparently, it isn't enough to
draw moviegoers.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:29.480
Ultimately, it's possible that the appetite
for new shared universe storytelling is starting

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:34.690
to wane, especially in comparison to properties
like those owned by Marvel and DC.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:38.130
It's not the first time a studio has failed
to kick-start a new franchise.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:42.870
In recent years, a good example is the infamous
Dark Universe, which pictured a future where

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:47.569
all of Universal's monster properties would
converge... but then 2017's "The Mummy" did

00:03:47.569 --> 00:03:49.540
little to inspire horror fans.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:54.250
There's also "The Amazing Spider-Man" debacle,
as Sony's attempt at creating its own separate

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:59.050
Marvel universe of Spidey-related characters
faltered following the poor reception to the

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:00.050
sequel.

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:03.850
Perhaps the financial and critical disappointment
of "Snake Eyes" might indicate to Paramount

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:07.960
that it's time to go back to the intellectual
property mine to find something else to adapt

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:09.290
for the big screen.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:13.230
If you're still curious about the film, "Snake
Eyes" is in theaters now.

